.TH LLDPND-CTL 8 "October 2026" "lldpnd" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
lldpnd-ctl \- query a running lldpnd
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B lldpnd\-ctl
.RB [ \-S
.IR path ]
.RB [ show | json | stats | local | help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B lldpnd\-ctl
connects to the control socket of
.BR lldpnd (8),
sends one command, and prints the reply.
.TP
.B show
Neighbour table of every port (default).
.TP
.B json
The complete state as one JSON object: chassis ID, system name and
description, capabilities, timers, and for each port its statistics and
neighbours (chassis and port IDs with their subtypes, TTL, time to
expiry, names, descriptions, capabilities, management addresses).
Octets received from the network that are not printable ASCII are
\\u\-escaped, so the output is always valid JSON.
.TP
.B stats
Per-port counters: frames out/in, discarded, in error, unrecognised
TLVs, ageouts, neighbours dropped because the table was full, and own
LLDPDUs seen (loops).
.TP
.B local
What this system advertises on each port.
.TP
.B help
List the commands.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-S " path"
Control socket (default
.IR /run/lldpnd.sock ;
the systemd unit uses
.IR /run/lldpnd/lldpnd.sock ).
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
lldpnd\-ctl \-S /run/lldpnd/lldpnd.sock json | jq '.ports[].neighbors[].system_name'
.fi
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success, 1 if the daemon cannot be reached, 2 on usage errors.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR lldpnd (8)
